This is a very common architectural challenge in high-volume financial systems. When you aggregate hundreds of thousands of transactions into a single batch, the totals can quickly overflow standard database constraints and cause the UI to truncate the numbers visually.
Here is the full-stack architectural standard for handling massively unbounded financial aggregates.
### 1. Database Data Type (Oracle)
You should use **NUMBER(38,4)** for aggregate total columns.
In Oracle, 38 is the absolute maximum precision you can assign to a NUMBER. By defining it as NUMBER(38,4), you reserve 4 digits for the decimal, leaving 34 digits for the whole number. This safely allows totals up to the decillions (a number with 34 zeros), ensuring your database will *never* throw a numeric overflow error, no matter how large the batch is.
Run this DDL to update your master table:
```sql
ALTER TABLE JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER MODIFY TOTAL_DEBIT NUMBER(38,4);
ALTER TABLE JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER MODIFY TOTAL_CREDIT NUMBER(38,4);

```
### 2. Java Model Data Type
Your Java entity and DTOs should strictly use **java.math.BigDecimal**.
Do not use Double or Long. BigDecimal dynamically allocates memory to perfectly represent the exact precision of the number coming from the database without any floating-point drift or maximum limits.
Ensure your DTO mapping looks like this:
```java
private BigDecimal totalDebit;
private BigDecimal totalCredit;

```
### 3. Frontend Adjustment (React DataGrid)
In your MUI DataGrid, a width of 150 is too small to display a 15+ digit number along with commas and 4 decimal places. You need to expand the column width to at least 220 or 250 to prevent UI truncation.
Find the totalDebit and totalCredit objects inside your columns array and update their widths:
**Existing Code:**
```javascript
    {
      field: "totalDebit",
      headerName: "Total Debit",
      width: 150, // <--- Too small
      align: "right",
      headerAlign: "right",
      disableColumnMenu: true,
      sortable: false,
      renderCell: (p) => (
        <Typography variant="body2" sx={{ mt: 1.5, fontWeight: 500 }}>
          {formatCurrency(p.value)}
        </Typography>
      ),
    },
    {
      field: "totalCredit",
      headerName: "Total Credit",
      width: 150, // <--- Too small
      align: "right",
      headerAlign: "right",
      disableColumnMenu: true,
      sortable: false,
      renderCell: (p) => (
        <Typography variant="body2" sx={{ mt: 1.5, fontWeight: 500 }}>
          {formatCurrency(p.value)}
        </Typography>
      ),
    },

```
**Replace with:**
```javascript
    {
      field: "totalDebit",
      headerName: "Total Debit",
      width: 220, // <--- Safely accommodates massive formatted numbers
      align: "right",
      headerAlign: "right",
      disableColumnMenu: true,
      sortable: false,
      renderCell: (p) => (
        <Tooltip title={p.value ? p.value.toString() : "0"} placement="top">
          <Typography variant="body2" sx={{ mt: 1.5, fontWeight: 500, whiteSpace: 'nowrap', overflow: 'hidden', textOverflow: 'ellipsis' }}>
            {formatCurrency(p.value)}
          </Typography>
        </Tooltip>
      ),
    },
    {
      field: "totalCredit",
      headerName: "Total Credit",
      width: 220, // <--- Safely accommodates massive formatted numbers
      align: "right",
      headerAlign: "right",
      disableColumnMenu: true,
      sortable: false,
      renderCell: (p) => (
        <Tooltip title={p.value ? p.value.toString() : "0"} placement="top">
          <Typography variant="body2" sx={{ mt: 1.5, fontWeight: 500, whiteSpace: 'nowrap', overflow: 'hidden', textOverflow: 'ellipsis' }}>
            {formatCurrency(p.value)}
          </Typography>
        </Tooltip>
      ),
    },

```
*Note: I also wrapped the text in a MUI <Tooltip>. If a number ever does manage to exceed the 220px width, the text will safely truncate with an ellipsis (...), and the user can simply hover over it to see the exact, full total in the tooltip!*
